  "body": {
    "id": "lem:factorization",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\), every \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\), and all real \\(s,t\\), \\(F_x(s,t)=\\widehat f_x(s+t)\\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t)\\) and \\(G_x(s,t)=\\widehat q_x(s+t)\\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(P\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal X\\)",
      "\\(F_x\\)",
      "\\(G_x\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat f_x\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat q_x\\)",
      "\\(\\phi_1\\)",
      "\\(\\phi_2\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:observable-channels",
      "ass:strata",
      "ass:error-latent-independence",
      "ass:error-coordinate-independence",
      "ass:compact-dose",
      "lem:structural-response-implications"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the observable two-channel factorization on which injectivity and inference rest.",
    "gap": "Capitao-Miniconi, Gassiat, and Lehéricy analyze only the first identity; the marked identity is absent from their model.",
    "consumer": "The empirical characteristic-function confidence set uses these residual equations jointly.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\), \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\), and \\(s,t\\in\\mathbb R\\).  Assumption \\(\\mathrm{ass{:}strata}\\) gives \\(p_x>0\\), so conditioning on \\(X=x\\) is well defined.  The replicate definitions imply\n\\[\n e^{i(sS_1+tS_2)}=e^{i(s+t)A}e^{isU_1}e^{itU_2}.\n\\]\nThe two independence assumptions therefore give\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n F_x(s,t)\n &=E_P[e^{i(s+t)A}\\mid X=x]E_P[e^{isU_1}]E_P[e^{itU_2}]\\\\\n &=\\left\\{\\int_{\\mathcal A}e^{i(s+t)a}f_x(a)\\,da\\right\\}\n       \\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t)\\\\\n &=\\widehat f_x(s+t)\\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor the marked channel, \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}structural\\text{-}response\\text{-}implications}\\) gives \\(|Y|\\le B_Y\\) almost surely, so all marked expectations are finite.  Error independence allows the bounded mark to remain with the latent variables, whence\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n G_x(s,t)\n &=E_P[Ye^{i(s+t)A}\\mid X=x]E_P[e^{isU_1}]E_P[e^{itU_2}]\\\\\n &=\\left\\{\\int_{\\mathcal A}e^{i(s+t)a}\n       E_P[Y\\mid A=a,X=x]f_x(a)\\,da\\right\\}\n       \\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t)\\\\\n &=\\widehat q_x(s+t)\\phi_1(s)\\phi_2(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere the definition \\(q_x=m_xf_x\\) justifies the last equality, and \\(\\mathrm{ass{:}compact\\text{-}dose}\\) justifies writing both integrals over \\(\\mathcal A\\).  This proves the two identities.",
